  "query_preview": "/Through Sunday/...\nIssued at 1247 PM MDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\nTemperatures this afternoon are already in the upper 90s to lower\n100s across the I-25 corridor and northern plains. The Heat\nAdvisory began at noon today and will go into tomorrow afternoon.\nOvernight temperatures will remain well above normal tonight, \npotentially reaching record-breaking values (if the front doesn't\ndrop temperatures below the morning low in the evening tomorrow).\nThe current forecast low for Denver tomorrow morning is 71 \ndegrees and the record warmest low for tomorrow's date is 68 \ndegrees, set in 1981.\n\nThe high temperature forecast for tomorrow still has quite a bit of \nuncertainty for our northern plains counties. The thermal ridge \nwill remain over the area, with anomalously warm 700mb \ntemperatures. Models are still struggling with the timing and \nstrength of tomorrow's cold front, changing the time it enters our\nforecast area from the north by as much as 6 hours from run to \nrun. If the front ends up moving into the area prior to peak \nheating, highs in our northern counties could be significantly \nreduced, and northern portions of our Heat Advisory could remain \nbelow Heat Advisory criteria. However, if the front takes on the \nslower approach, temperatures could reach into the upper 90s to \nlower 100s range for our northern counties. Confidence increases\nin our high temperature forecast the further south we go, \nincluding the urban corridor. Right now, we're going to leave the\nspatial extent of the Heat Advisory the same, but we did trim off\na few hours at the end of the Advisory to account for the cooling\nbehind the front. \n\nNow for the precip side of things tomorrow. There is a low chance\n(~20% to 30%) that the front could trigger some convective \nactivity tomorrow afternoon. Lower levels however will be very \ndry, so if the surface convergence and upslope from the \nnortheasterly winds can trigger some activity, rain may not \nactually be able to get to the surface before evaporating. Which \nmeans we could be dealing with some strong, gusty winds due to \nvirga and DCAPEs around 1300 to 2000 J/kg in the afternoon. We'll \nalso have to be on the lookout for spotty critical fire weather \nconditions tomorrow. Relative humidities will be low ahead of the \nfront, with some recovery behind it. However, the gusty winds \nassociated with the front could overlap with the low RHs for an \nhour or two before the RHs can recover enough to get out of those\ncritical thresholds. No RFW will be issued at this time due to \nthe spotty nature of the critical conditions, in both time and \nspace, but it is something to be cautious of. \n\nTemperatures will be cooler behind this frontal system for \nTuesday and Wednesday. The strength of the front is till a bit up\nin the air, but the good news (for those tired of the heat), is \nthat the NBM 25th to 75th percentile for highs on Tuesday generally\nranges from the mid 70s to mid 80s in the plains. So, confidence \nis pretty high that temperatures will be at least 15 degrees \ncooler for much of the plains on Tuesday, if not closer to 25 \ndegrees cooler. The mountains won't be as lucky however. The \nfront will have a smaller impact in the higher terrain, as that \nthermal ridge lurks off to our west. Models are showing a \ndisturbance moving through the flow aloft Tuesday \nafternoon/evening. This disturbance and easterly, upslope surface \nwinds could be enough to trigger some convection. Models are \nshowing some mid level capping, which could prevent any strong \nstorms from developing. But, if we can get over the cap, \nstrengthening mid level winds will allow for decent wind shear and\nthe potential for stronger storms. \n\nTemperatures will gradually warm each afternoon into the weekend, \nwith highs reaching into the triple digits by Friday or Saturday for \nportions of the plains. Precipitation chances will decrease as we go \ninto the end of the work week and start of the weekend, as the upper \nlevel high builds back over the area and large scale subsidence \ntakes over once again.",
